nodecode-freight is a rewrite of harrylabsj's harrylabsj/hermes-plugin-freight-reconciliation:

  https://github.com/harrylabsj/hermes-plugin-freight-reconciliation
  commit 160ed80ddf6333e48f176f299322aaf9ed2414b0
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Haina (harrylabsj) (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the payer-side reconciliation itself, in Common Lisp instead of the
original's Python engine. A case is one carrier, one currency, one billing
period; the five inputs (bill lines, trips, rate rules, waiting evidence, toll
receipts) are imported from CSV and frozen in the case file with the digest of
the file they came from; matching is the original's P1 rule (the carrier's own
trip_ref names a trip of the ledger, a ref to another carrier's trip is a
conflict); the rate in force is selected from the confirmed rules by carrier,
route, vehicle class, charge code and the trip's departure, with the same
RATE_NOT_FOUND / RATE_AMBIGUOUS refusal; the three formulas are the original's
(per trip, waiting blocks with the free seconds taken off first and the rest
rounded up, actual receipt with an optional cap); amounts are integer minor
units parsed from decimal strings, and a charge with no comparable rate keeps
`unknown` rather than zero; the issue codes, the duplicate and reversal checks,
the three partitions that add up to the bill total, the not-billed trips, and
the per-group evidence references are the original's. The original's habit of
never approving a payment is kept: the cell drafts a difference list and an
export, and a person decides.

Left out, because each belongs to the Hermes host or to the original's bigger
engine: the 14 MCP tools are five tools here (open, import, run, differences,
export), and the local admin page a person used to confirm on has no
counterpart — a Nodecode cell answers in the session, so the confirmation is
the person reading the export; the SQLite schema with its job queue, the
crash-recovery pass that marks interrupted runs, and the paged evidence views
are replaced by one JSON case file and a direct call; the alias matching (P2)
and the fuzzy candidate list (P3) are left to a person, who can re-import a
corrected trip_ref; the rate-rule brief versions, the confirmed-object workflow
and the xlsx export are not carried (the export is CSV); the Chinese interface
and the bilingual skills are not carried (the manual is the English primer on
(help :freight)); and the original's six-file limit and row caps are a single
`max_rows` setting.
